@@@ changelog
# Changelog

Format: [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), versioning: [SemVer](https://semver.org/).
The file format has its own version (`%KAPU 1.1`, see `SPEC-KAPU.md`); the tools below are versioned separately.

## [Unreleased]

### Changed
- Search by meaning uses one vector per window of a door's text (at most 512 tokens, overlapping by an eighth); the best window counts (D141). Room-level recall@10 on real questions, hybrid: Rust book 0.46 to 0.49, Kubernetes docs 0.44 to 0.46; on MDN, with its many short sections, 0.28 to 0.27. The vector cache (`.vecs2`) keeps the windows of unchanged doors after a write, and `kapu import`, `build`, `add` and `rm` compute them right away.
- File format 1.1 (specification v1.1, D140): an `#INDEX` posting is `<gap>:<tf>`, the gap between the doors' line numbers in `#DOCS`, instead of the door id. Files are about 4.5 times smaller (Kubernetes docs: 117 MB to 26 MB, MDN: 542 MB to 128 MB) and the 13-million-token MDN corpus builds within the 1 MiB line limit. Format 1.0 files are still read; `kapu build` rewrites them as 1.1.
- Specification v1.0.5 (D139): one call parses and checks the file once, a term's `#INDEX` line is found by binary search, and the MCP server keeps a checked copy of an unchanged file. Search on the Kubernetes docs: 657 ms to 19 ms (median), on MDN 3.2 s to 158 ms; opening a door: 216 ms to 5 ms.
- Specification v1.0.4 (D131-D138): group names leave out parameter-table words; the search excerpt prefers rarer query words; the map's totals line names the content language (`lang=`) and the guide asks for queries in that language; at most 10 content-scan lines on the map (`scan=true` lists all); `kapu_open` serves long doors in parts (`max_tokens`, `part`); the MCP server keeps unchanged files and door vectors in memory.
- Specification v1.0.2: in-page anchors link to the room's own sections and no longer warn (D123); group names leave out stop words (D121).
- `kapu-mcp`: tool annotations, at most 50 hits, a next step in every error (D120); an excerpt line under each search hit (D124); a group lists its rooms only and `room=` lists one room's sub-doors (D125); at most 8 neighbours per opened door (D126); a totals line on the map (D127).

### Removed
- The internal planning documents (status log, decision log, open questions, milestone plan, research plans, early concept archive) left the repository; decisions are still cited by their D-numbers in the specification and the code.

### Fixed
- Invisible characters (U+200B-U+200D, U+2060, U+FEFF) in a room's title or first sentence no longer produce a file the reader rejects (D135).
- `kapu ... | head` no longer panics when the reader closes the pipe early (D138).
- The S5 content scan no longer matches a command name inside another word (`async`, `confirm`) (D122).

### Added
- `examples/kapu-docs.kapu`: this documentation as a KAPU file, the quickstart example (`tools/make_examples.py`).
- The results page `docs/results/index.html` (English and Hungarian, written by `tools/study/results_page.py` from `bench/results/`) and the animated terminal demo `docs/assets/demo.svg` (`tools/make_demo_svg.py`).
- Real-corpus measurements: `bench/RESULTS.md`, `bench/results/`.
- `kapu bench --json`: room-level recall@5/10 and MRR@10, where any door of the expected room is a hit (D130).
- Study pilot tools in `tools/study/`: corpus preparation from public documentation, Stack Overflow questions with a gold page, chunk-RAG baselines, scale probe, logged file-search, llms.txt and RAG tools for agents.
- Tools: `tools/collect_skills.py`, `tools/mcp_probe.py`, `tools/kapu_mcp_call.py`, `tools/mcp_eval.py`.

## [0.1.0] - 2026-10-23

First portfolio release. Specification v1.0.1, milestones M0-M7 and M10 done, M8 and M9 in part.

### Added
- `kapu-core`: reader, validator (error codes E001-E012), builder with BM25 index, token prices (o200k_base), YAKE tags, BLAKE3 fingerprints, graph (`link`, `term`, `sim` edges), Leiden groups and hybrid search (BM25 + static embeddings, RRF).
- `kapu` command line tool: `new`, `import`, `export`, `add`, `rm`, `build`, `map`, `search`, `describe`, `open`, `validate`, `bench`; every command with `--json`.
- `kapu-mcp`: MCP server over stdio with `kapu_map`, `kapu_search`, `kapu_describe`, `kapu_open`, `kapu_write`, the std-1 guide and the content scan (FR-19).
- `kapu-gui`: desktop window (Tauri 2) with the map and token prices, room reader, search with a token budget, graph view and file checks.
- Demo file `examples/kapu-spec.kapu`: the specification packed as a KAPU file.
- Author signature `psz // v0.1.0`: last line of `kapu --help`, app info card on `--version` and in the window.
- Packed vector model: `potion-multilingual-128M` in int8 (124 MB), portable Windows package, draft release workflow for Linux and macOS.
- Byte-level Python reference builder, test vectors, cross-checks, fuzz targets, CI on Windows, Linux and macOS.

@@@ fuzzing
# Fuzz targets (NFR-03/5)

`cargo-fuzz` (libFuzzer) targets for every parser of `kapu-core`. The goal is
that no input, however broken, makes the library panic, overflow or read out
of bounds: every input must end in `Ok` or an `Err` with an FR-20 code.

| Target | What it feeds |
|---|---|
| `header` | any bytes into `Header::parse` |
| `sections` | whole file, strict and tolerant, tight limits, door reads |
| `docs` | `#DOCS` lines, unescape, comment stripping, served text |
| `catalog` | `#MAP`, `#DESC`, `#GRAPH` line parsers |
| `validate` | whole file, basic and full validation |
| `reader` | whole file, open every door plus unknown ids, describe, frame |

Run (nightly is needed only here; the seed corpus is the M1 test vectors):

```
mkdir fuzz/corpus/<target>; cp tests/vectors/*.kapu fuzz/corpus/<target>/
cargo +nightly fuzz run <target> fuzz/corpus/<target> -- -max_total_time=3600
```

Release gate: one hour per target without a finding.
`fuzz/corpus` and `fuzz/artifacts` are git-ignored.

@@@ overview
# KAPU overview

> On a 207,000-token manual the model had never seen, Claude agents using KAPU answered **12 of 12** beginner questions while reading **4,104 tokens** per question: **half** of what the same agents read with grep over the Markdown file, and **2%** of pasting the whole manual into the context.

## Results

The same 12 questions about the PixelLab documentation (third-party, not published), the same prompt for every format, three blind judges checking every claim against the documentation:

| How the agent got the knowledge | Tokens read per question (median) | Correct of 12 |
|---|---:|---:|
| **KAPU** (map, search, open) | **4,104** | **12** |
| One Markdown file, grep and line reads | 8,280 | 12 |
| The same text as a PDF, search and page reads | 8,207 | 11 |
| The whole file pasted into the context | 206,899 | – |

On public documentation with real Stack Overflow questions (format 1.1, one Windows 11 machine, release build):

| Corpus | Knowledge | `.kapu` file | Search | Open a section | Retrieval: KAPU / chunked BM25 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust book | 0.3 M tokens | 2.2 MB | 13 ms | 2 ms | 0.49 / 0.49 |
| Kubernetes docs | 3.9 M tokens | 26 MB | 19 ms | 5 ms | 0.46 / 0.45 |
| MDN Web Docs | 13.0 M tokens | 128 MB | 158 ms | 44 ms | 0.27 / 0.30 |

Retrieval is room-level recall@10: the page the accepted answer links to is among the first 10 hits. Times are medians over the MCP server. Full results with charts, method and limits: **[results page](https://piszabolcs.github.io/kapu/results/)** ([source](docs/results/index.html)) and [bench/RESULTS.md](bench/RESULTS.md); raw numbers in [bench/results/](bench/results/).

## Why

AI agents get their working knowledge as Markdown: a manual, a specification, a library of skills, project notes. Pasting all of it costs tokens on every turn and pushes the useful part out of the context window. A vector database needs a server and an embedding service, cuts the text into chunks and loses the structure. Many small files lose the overview, and the agent has to guess which one to read.

KAPU keeps the Markdown as it is and adds, in the same file, a map of every section with its token price, a search index, a graph of related sections and fingerprints. The agent sees the table of contents first and pays only for what it opens.

| | A folder of Markdown | Vector database / RAG | KAPU |
|---|---|---|---|
| What you run | nothing | a database and an embedding service | one binary; the 124 MB embedding model is optional and offline |
| What the agent sees first | file names | nothing until it queries | a map with every section and its token price |
| What the agent reads | whole files, or grep lines without context | chunks cut at a fixed size | whole sections it picks |
| Structure | files and headings | lost in the chunks | rooms, sections, links and topic groups |
| In git | yes | no, a binary index | yes: one UTF-8 text file, byte-identical rebuilds |

## Quickstart

Build from source with [Rust](https://rustup.rs) (the toolchain is pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/piszabolcs/kapu.git
cd kapu
cargo build --release -p kapu-cli -p kapu-mcp
```

Look around in the example, which is this project's own documentation as a KAPU file:

```bash
target/release/kapu map examples/kapu-docs.kapu
target/release/kapu search examples/kapu-docs.kapu "how do I connect it to Claude Code" --k 3
target/release/kapu open examples/kapu-docs.kapu overview/use-it-from-claude-code
```

Make your own from a folder of Markdown files (one room per file, `##` headings become sections):

```bash
target/release/kapu import ./my-notes notes.kapu
```

Search by meaning is optional. For it, download [`minishlab/potion-multilingual-128M`](https://huggingface.co/minishlab/potion-multilingual-128M) (`config.json`, `README.md`, `tokenizer.json`, `model.safetensors`) into `models/potion-multilingual-128M` and make the int8 copy the tools look for: `python tools/quantize.py models/potion-multilingual-128M`. Without it, keyword search (BM25) still works.

## Use it from Claude Code

```bash
claude mcp add kapu -- /path/to/kapu-mcp
```

Then ask, for example: "Use `notes.kapu` to answer: how do we deploy the staging server?" The server gives the agent five tools:

| Tool | What it returns |
|---|---|
| `kapu_map` | the map: groups, rooms and their token prices, the content language, a short guide on first use |
| `kapu_search` | the best sections for a query, each with its price and the line that matched |
| `kapu_describe` | the description and tags of a section, without opening it |
| `kapu_open` | the text of a section, framed so it cannot pose as instructions; long sections in parts |
| `kapu_write` | adds a room from Markdown, rebuilds the file and reports what the content scan found |

For Claude Desktop or another MCP client, start `kapu-mcp` over stdio; `KAPU_MODEL_DIR` points it at the model folder. Keep the `.kapu` file outside the agent's working directory if you want it to use the tools instead of reading the file directly.

## How it works

A `.kapu` file is plain UTF-8 text. The Markdown body is the single source of truth; every other section is rebuilt from it, byte for byte, by `kapu build`.

```text
%KAPU 1.1          512-byte header: where each section starts, fingerprints, counts
#MAP    ... #END   groups and doors (rooms and their sections) with token prices
#DESC   ... #END   one-line descriptions and tags
#GRAPH  ... #END   links, shared rare terms and similar meaning between doors
#INDEX  ... #END   BM25 index; postings are positions in #DOCS, not ids
#DOCS   ... #END   byte offset, length, BLAKE3 fingerprint and word count of every door
#BODY              the Markdown rooms, one after the other
```

- **Map first.** Even for 13 million tokens of documentation the map is about 2,000 tokens: groups (Leiden clusters named by their keywords) and their rooms with prices.
- **Hybrid search.** BM25 with English and Hungarian stemming, plus a static multilingual embedding model with one vector per 512-token window of a section, merged with reciprocal rank fusion.
- **Opening is a seek.** The reader jumps to the byte offset in `#DOCS`, checks the section's fingerprint and returns its text. A section longer than 2,000 tokens comes in parts.
- **Edits stay safe.** If someone edits the body by hand, the fingerprints no longer match; the reader notices, rebuilds the positions from the room markers and reports a stale index until the next build.
- **Content scan.** Writing a room warns about text that tries to override an agent's instructions, hidden characters, long encoded blocks and unexpected download commands. It warns, it does not block.

The format is specified in [SPEC-KAPU.md](SPEC-KAPU.md) (in Hungarian for now), with test vectors for every error code in [tests/vectors](tests/vectors) and a byte-level Python reference builder in [tools/build_vectors.py](tools/build_vectors.py).

## Desktop window

`kapu-gui` shows a file the way an agent sees it: map, rooms, search, graph and checks.

```bash
cargo build --release -p kapu-gui
target/release/kapu-gui examples/kapu-docs.kapu
```

On Linux the window needs WebKitGTK: `sudo apt install libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev libxdo-dev libssl-dev librsvg2-dev`.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph Input
    MD["Markdown files"]
    MOD["potion-multilingual-128M (int8, optional)"]
  end
  subgraph Core["kapu-core"]
    B["Builder: index, prices, graph, groups"]
    F[".kapu file"]
    R["Reader and validator"]
    S["Hybrid search (BM25 + window vectors, RRF)"]
  end
  subgraph Tools
    CLI["kapu (CLI)"]
    MCP["kapu-mcp (stdio)"]
    GUI["kapu-gui (Tauri)"]
  end
  AG["AI agent"]
  MD --> B --> F --> R --> S
  MOD --> B
  MOD --> S
  R --> CLI & MCP & GUI
  S --> CLI & MCP & GUI
  MCP --> AG
```

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Language | Rust 1.99 (edition 2024), no `unsafe` |
| File format | UTF-8 text, 512-byte header, BLAKE3 fingerprints, token prices with `tiktoken-rs` (o200k_base) |
| Search | BM25 with Snowball stemming (`rust-stemmers`), Model2Vec static embeddings (`tokenizers`, safetensors), RRF |
| Graph and groups | `link`, `term` and `sim` edges, own Leiden implementation (checked against `leidenalg`), YAKE group names |
| Agent interface | MCP over stdio with `rmcp` |
| Command line and window | `clap` 4; Tauri 2 with plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript |
| Quality | clippy pedantic, cargo-deny, cargo-audit, cargo-fuzz, CI on Windows, Linux and macOS, a byte-level Python reference builder |

## Project structure

```text
kapu/
├── crates/
│   ├── kapu-core/    format, builder, reader, validator, search, graph, groups
│   ├── kapu-cli/     the `kapu` command line tool
│   ├── kapu-mcp/     the `kapu-mcp` MCP server (stdio)
│   ├── kapu-gui/     the `kapu-gui` desktop window (Tauri, ui/ is the interface)
│   └── kapu-bench/   synthetic corpus generator and measurements
├── examples/         kapu-docs.kapu (this documentation), kapu-spec.kapu (the specification)
├── tests/            test vectors, one file per error code; the tiny test model
├── bench/            RESULTS.md and the raw results as JSON
├── docs/             results page, logo, screenshots, demo
├── fuzz/             cargo-fuzz targets
├── tools/            Python reference builder, quantizer, measurement scripts
└── scripts/          check, fuzz and package scripts
```

## Status

KAPU is a working pre-release: the format, the command line, the MCP server and the Windows window are in daily use; the measurements above are a pilot, not a benchmark.

- [ ] Prebuilt binaries for Windows, Linux and macOS from the release workflow
- [ ] The specification in English
- [ ] Paging for the room list of very large groups (13M-token corpora)
- [ ] Better search when the question and the text are in different languages
- [ ] The desktop window tested by hand on Linux and macOS

## License

Code: MIT, see [LICENSE](LICENSE). Specification (`SPEC-KAPU.md`): CC BY 4.0. Fonts in `crates/kapu-gui/ui/fonts`: SIL Open Font License 1.1. Third-party crates and the model: [THIRD-PARTY.md](THIRD-PARTY.md).

@@@ python-tools
# Developer-only tools (not shipped)

- `build_vectors.py`: builds `tests/vectors/` (the M1 sample file, one invalid
  file per error code, two reader edge cases and `expected.json`). It is the
  executable reference for every byte-level rule the spec leaves open
  (decisions D56-D66); the Rust builder must reproduce `minta.kapu` byte
  for byte (M3 check).
- `blake3_ref.py`: pure-Python BLAKE3 reference used by the script.
- `crosscheck.py`: builds a Markdown folder with the reference and compares it,
  section by section, with the Rust build.
- `quantize.py`: makes the int8 copy of the vector model the tools look for.
- `make_examples.py`: rebuilds `examples/kapu-docs.kapu` (this documentation)
  and `examples/kapu-spec.kapu` (the specification, via `split_spec.py`).
- `make_demo_svg.py`: writes `docs/assets/demo.svg`, the animated terminal in
  the README, from real `kapu map`, `search` and `open` output.
- `study/`: the measurement scripts of `bench/RESULTS.md` chapters 8 and 9;
  `study/results_page.py` writes `docs/results/index.html` from the results JSON.
- `mcp_probe.py`, `mcp_eval.py`, `kapu_mcp_call.py`: MCP latency, isolated
  agent runs and a logged one-call client for agents.

Setup (Windows, from the repository root):

```
py -3.13 -m venv tools/.venv
tools/.venv/Scripts/python -m pip install -r tools/requirements.txt
tools/.venv/Scripts/python tools/build_vectors.py
```

`tiktoken` downloads the `o200k_base` vocabulary on first use.

@@@ results
# KAPU measurements

English summary of the measurements. Raw numbers: [results/2026-10-23-english-corpus.json](results/2026-10-23-english-corpus.json). Chapter 8 is a pilot on public documentation, with its own results file; chapter 9 measures it again with file format 1.1. The [results page](../docs/results/index.html) shows the pilot and format 1.1 with charts.

- **Date:** 2026-10-23
- **Version:** tools 0.1.0 with specification v1.0.2
- **Machine:** Windows 11 Pro, release build (`lto`, `codegen-units = 1`)
- **Vector model:** `potion-multilingual-128M`, int8 (124 MB)

## Summary

| What | Result |
|---|---|
| Knowledge in the test file | 2.35 million characters, 573,733 tokens of Markdown, 244 rooms |
| Live run: Claude agents that could only use the KAPU tools | 10 of 10 questions answered correctly |
| Tokens an agent read per question in the live run (median) | 842 (1.5 tool calls) |
| Map call, the agent's first look at the file | 220 tokens (777 on the first call of a session, with the guide) |
| Search with 5 hits and an excerpt line under each (median) | 333 tokens, 280 ms |
| Retrieval, 77 English questions, hybrid search | recall@5 0.84, recall@10 0.90, MRR@10 0.67 |
| Build with the vector model / full check with rebuild | 4.6 s / 3.3 s; the second build is byte-identical |

An agent never reads the `.kapu` file itself: 33.0 million characters, 9.8 million tokens, 90% of it the search index that the tools read. The fair comparison is with the knowledge itself, the 573,733 tokens of Markdown, which do not fit into any current context window. In the live run an agent found its answer after reading a median of 842 tokens, about 1/680 of the knowledge.

## 1. Test corpus

A local corpus, not published, because most of it is third-party text:

- **216 rooms:** the Claude skills installed on the author's machine. These are Anthropic's skills, the superpowers, Figma and claude-md-management plugins, and the author's own skills, each as a room, with its reference documents as separate rooms. `tools/collect_skills.py` collects them, taking the newest version of each plugin.
- **28 rooms:** modern frontend sample code (CSS, the web platform, React 19, Tailwind 4, accessibility, performance). An agent wrote them, checking browser support against MDN, web.dev and caniuse.

Hungarian rooms were left out, so this measures an English knowledge file with English questions.

| Part of the file | Characters | Tokens (o200k_base) |
|---|---:|---:|
| Body: the Markdown knowledge | 2,350,156 | 573,733 |
| Map, descriptions, graph, positions | 1,056,712 | 347,852 |
| Search index | 29,569,515 | 8,887,658 |
| Whole `.kapu` file | 32,976,901 | 9,809,442 |

Build: 244 rooms, 1,916 doors (rooms and their `##` sections), 10 groups, 4.6 s with the vector model. A second build gives the same bytes, the full check (fingerprints plus a rebuild comparison) passes in 3.3 s, and there are no warnings.

## 2. What an agent pays

Measured through the official Python MCP client in one session (`tools/mcp_probe.py`):

| Call | Tokens returned |
|---|---:|
| `kapu_map`, first call of a session (guide, totals line, 10 groups, content-scan notes) | 777 |
| `kapu_map`, later calls (totals line and 10 groups) | 220 |
| `kapu_map group=...`, one group's rooms | 399 - 2,005 (all 10 groups: 9,698) |
| `kapu_map room=...`, one room's sections | 760 (example) |
| `kapu_search`, 5 hits with excerpts (median / p90) | 333 / 382 |
| `kapu_open`, one door (median / p90) | 1,655 / 5,401 |
| `kapu_describe`, one door (median) | 53 |

## 3. Retrieval quality

There are 77 English questions:

- 49 point at skill rooms and 28 at frontend rooms.
- 18 of them point at one `##` section, not a whole room.

They are phrased as a developer's need, and where a paraphrase works they avoid the room's title and the API's name. Their authors knew the content; see Limitations.

- **Strict:** the expected door is in the top k.
- **Lenient:** the expected door or its room is in the top k.

| Mode | recall@5 | recall@10 | MRR@10 | lenient recall@10 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| BM25 (keywords, Snowball stemming) | 0.83 | 0.90 | 0.68 | 0.95 |
| Vector (static embeddings) | 0.64 | 0.73 | 0.47 | 0.79 |
| Hybrid (reciprocal rank fusion) | 0.84 | 0.90 | 0.67 | 0.94 |

| Questions | n | Hybrid recall@5 | Hybrid recall@10 | Hybrid MRR@10 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Skill rooms | 49 | 0.80 | 0.86 | 0.65 |
| Frontend rooms | 28 | 0.93 | 0.96 | 0.71 |
| Whole room expected | 59 | 0.92 | 0.93 | 0.76 |
| One section expected | 18 | 0.61 | 0.78 | 0.40 |

On English text with English questions, keyword search alone is already strong, and the hybrid ranking keeps that level. The vector model matters when the question and the text are in different languages. That is a separate track: Hungarian questions over English text reach a hybrid recall@10 of 0.45.

## 4. MCP server

| Tool | Median | p90 | Notes |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| initialize | 31 ms | | |
| `kapu_map` | 80 ms | 83 ms | the whole 33 MB file is read and checked on every call |
| `kapu_search` (k = 5, with excerpts) | 280 ms | 293 ms | the first search of a session is 1.5 s, while the vector model loads |
| `kapu_open` | 71 ms | 91 ms | |
| `kapu_describe` | 70 ms | 91 ms | |

All five tools carry MCP annotations: the four read tools are `readOnlyHint`, and `kapu_write` is `destructiveHint`. Errors name the next step, for example: `E009: unknown door ... (next: take door ids from kapu_map door lines or from kapu_search)`.

## 5. Live evaluation

The evaluation followed the format of the mcp-builder skill: 10 questions, each with one verifiable answer. The topics and every call are in the results JSON.

- **Agents:** five Claude Sonnet agents, two questions each.
- **Tool access:** only the four read tools, through `tools/kapu_mcp_call.py`, which speaks real MCP to `kapu-mcp` and logs every call. The agents were not allowed to read the file.

| # | Topic | Correct | Calls | Tools | Tokens read |
|---:|---|:---:|---:|---|---:|
| 1 | design reference: recommended button colour | yes | 3 | search, search, open | 1,785 |
| 2 | debugging: phase before any fix | yes | 2 | search, open | 2,714 |
| 3 | spreadsheet formula check script | yes | 2 | search, open | 907 |
| 4 | MCP pagination default | yes | 3 | search, map room, open | 1,280 |
| 5 | README badge limit | yes | 3 | search, open, search | 1,210 |
| 6 | TypeScript MCP SDK package name | yes | 1 | search | 373 |
| 7 | total number of rooms | yes | 1 | map | 777 |
| 8 | CSS anchor positioning: browser version | yes | 1 | search | 355 |
| 9 | `scheduler.yield()`: missing browser | yes | 1 | search | 337 |
| 10 | `field-sizing`: Firefox version | yes | 1 | search | 334 |

Five of the ten answers came from a search excerpt alone, without opening a door. The agents asked for two things:

- an excerpt that shows the answer line more often (questions 4 and 5);
- fewer neighbour lines when a large room is opened.

## 6. Changes made from these measurements

An earlier run on a mixed English and Hungarian corpus led to specification v1.0.2:

| Change | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Group names left out stop words (D121) | "the, and, css" | "css, rem, element" |
| Content-scan network rule matches whole commands only (D122) | 83 false warnings, first map 2,971 tokens | 4 real warnings, first map 777 tokens |
| In-page anchors link to their own section (D123) | 274 false "missing door" warnings | 0 warnings, 113 new link edges |
| An excerpt line under each search hit (D124) | median 2,238 tokens per live question | median 842 (different corpus, 8 of 10 questions the same) |
| A group lists its rooms; `room=` lists one room's sections (D125) | largest group list 15,756 tokens | 2,005 tokens |
| At most 8 neighbours per opened door (D126) | up to 31 neighbour lines | 8 lines and a count |
| Totals line on the map (D127) | the room count took two calls | one call |

## 7. Limitations

- The questions were written by people (and an agent) who had seen the content. This is a first measurement, not a benchmark; an independent question set is still needed.
- No baselines yet: full-context reading where it fits, chunk-based RAG, `grep`-style agentic file search and `llms.txt`.
- One machine, one vector model, one agent model (Claude Sonnet), ten live questions.
- The wrapper of the live run starts a new server for every call, so the guide and the content-scan notes appear more often than in a real session. Token counts are therefore slightly high.
- The runs used specification v1.0.2, whose tool output still had a few Hungarian labels ("szomszédok", "alajtó"); since v1.0.3 all agent-facing output is English.

## 8. Pilot on public documentation

This is a first run of the study design. Raw numbers are in [results/2026-10-23-study-pilot.json](results/2026-10-23-study-pilot.json).

**Corpora:**
- S: The Rust Programming Language, 0.32M tokens;
- M: the Kubernetes docs, 3.9M tokens;
- L: MDN Web Docs, 13.0M tokens.

**Questions:** real Stack Overflow questions whose accepted answer links exactly one page of the corpus. There are 68, 201 and 395 of them.

**Run setup:** Claude Sonnet agents, Sonnet and Haiku judges, one run.

### Scale

| | S | M | L |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Rooms / doors / groups | 111 / 231 / 7 | 1,722 / 6,370 / 104 | 14,719 / 84,910 / 99 |
| Build, peak memory | 3.4 s, 0.7 GB | 24 s, 1.4 GB | 190 s, 4.0 GB * |
| File size | 7.6 MB | 117 MB | 542 MB * |
| Search response (tokens, ms) | 249, 58 | 265, 666 | 213, 3,192 * |
| Map (tokens) | 151 | 1,948 | 1,919 * |
| Largest group list (tokens) | 804 | 6,957 | 30,118 * |

\* L does not build with the released tools: one search-index line of a common word exceeds the 1 MiB line limit. The L column comes from an experimental build with a 64 MiB limit.

What this shows:
- A search response stays around 200-270 tokens at every size.
- The map and the latency grow with the file, because the server reads the whole file on every call.
- These are open items for the next specification version.

### Retrieval without an agent

The table gives room-level recall@10. A hit is any door or chunk of the gold page in the top 10 units.

| | KAPU hybrid | KAPU BM25 | KAPU vector | 512-token chunks, BM25 | 512-token chunks, vector |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| S (68) | 0.46 | 0.44 | 0.32 | 0.49 | 0.43 |
| M (201) | 0.44 | 0.45 | 0.28 | 0.45 | 0.32 |
| L (395) | 0.28 | 0.28 | 0.15 | 0.30 | 0.19 |

- KAPU's hybrid search is on par with BM25 over chunks.
- Its vector search is weaker than chunk vectors, because one static vector per long door blurs the content.

### Agents on S, 30 questions

| System | Correct (Sonnet / Haiku judge) | Tokens read per question (median) |
|---|---:|---:|
| Closed book, no tools | 30 / 28 | 0 |
| Agentic chunk RAG | 30 / 27 | 4,150 |
| grep/read on the Markdown folder | 29 / 28 | 1,290 |
| llms.txt | 30 / 29 | 8,739 |
| KAPU | 30 / 29 | 3,983 |

The model answers these popular questions from memory, so accuracy cannot separate the systems. On a small, well-known corpus, grep reads the fewest tokens.

### Same content as KAPU, one Markdown file and a PDF

This test uses the PixelLab knowledge file, content the model does not know: 0.21M tokens and 12 beginner requests. The answers were graded blind against the documentation.

| Format | Correct | Supported claims | Tokens read per request (median) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| KAPU | 12/12 | 122/123 | 2,624 |
| One `.md` file, with search and line reads | 12/12 | 134/138 | 8,280 |
| PDF (219 pages), with search and page reads | 11/12 | 146/151 | 8,207 |
| Whole `.md` pasted into the conversation | - | - | 206,899 |

Accuracy is the same. KAPU read about a third of the tokens of the searchable file formats, and about 1/79 of pasting the whole file.

### After specification v1.0.4

A first set of fixes followed the pilot (D131-D138, specification chapter 17). The most visible ones:
- long doors open in parts of at most 2,000 tokens;
- at most 10 content-scan lines on the map;
- the content language on the totals line;
- an in-memory file and vector cache in the server.

Measured again with the same corpora and questions:

| | Before | After |
|---|---:|---:|
| KAPU agents on S, tokens read per question (median), correct | 3,983, 30/30 | 2,331, 30/30 |
| KAPU agents on S, tokens read for all 30 questions | 152,151 | 74,916 |
| First map call on M / L (tokens) | 4,989 / 4,139 | 2,318 / 2,303 |
| Search on M / L (ms, median) | 666 / 3,192 | 657 / 3,172 |

The PixelLab run was repeated with the same prompt for all three formats:

| Format | Correct | Supported claims | Tokens read per request (median) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| KAPU | 12/12 | 153/157 | 4,104 |
| One `.md` file | 12/12 | 140/145 | 8,280 |
| PDF | 11/12 | 147/151 | 8,207 |

- Splitting long doors halved what the agents read on the Rust book, at the same accuracy.
- The cache did not make search faster. The time goes to parsing the sections three times per search and to a linear scan of the search index, not to reading the file. That is the next fix.

## 9. File format 1.1 (specification v1.1)

Three changes after the pilot (D139-D141, specification chapters 18 and 19), measured on 2026-10-24 with the same corpora and questions:
- **Compact index (format 1.1).** An `#INDEX` posting names a door by the gap between line numbers in `#DOCS` instead of its id. Files are about 4.5 times smaller, and the longest index line stays under the 1 MiB line limit, so MDN builds with the released tool.
- **One parse per call.** A call parses and checks the file once (it was three times per search), finds a term's index line by binary search, and the MCP server keeps a checked copy of an unchanged file.
- **Window vectors.** Search by meaning uses one vector per 512-token window of a door's text instead of one vector for its first 512 tokens; the best window counts. `kapu import` and `kapu build` compute them, so the first search does not wait.

| | Rust book (S) | Kubernetes (M) | MDN (L) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| File size (MB), v1.0.4 → v1.1 | 7.6 → 2.2 | 117.4 → 26.2 | 542.2* → 128.3 |
| Builds with the released tool | yes | yes | no (E005) → yes |
| Import with the vector model (s) | 3.3 → 4.6 | 23.9 → 38.0 | 357.8* → 240.3 |
| Peak memory of the import (GB) | 0.70 → 0.69 | 1.35 → 1.03 | 3.87* → 2.68 |
| Search over MCP (ms, median) | 56 → 12.8 | 657 → 19.2 | 3,172 → 158.2 |
| Opening a door over MCP (ms, median) | 17 → 2.4 | 216 → 4.9 | 1,040 → 44.1 |
| Map over MCP (ms, median) | 18 → 3.0 | 253 → 46.6 | 1,400 → 390.9 |
| First search of a session (s) | 1.1 → 1.1 | 1.7 → 1.0 | 4.3 → 1.3 |
| `kapu bench` run time (s) | – → 1 | 187 → 8 | 1,774 → 127 |

\* MDN with v1.0.4: an experimental build with a 64 MiB line limit, and its import time was not measured on an idle machine. The import now includes the window vectors.

Retrieval on the same Stack Overflow questions, room-level recall@10 (MRR@10 in brackets):

| | Rust book, 68 questions | Kubernetes, 201 | MDN, 395 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| KAPU hybrid, v1.0.4 | 0.456 (0.207) | 0.438 (0.227) | 0.281 (0.131) |
| **KAPU hybrid, v1.1** | **0.485 (0.281)** | **0.458 (0.245)** | **0.271 (0.128)** |
| KAPU vectors only, v1.0.4 → v1.1 | 0.324 → 0.426 | 0.279 → 0.318 | 0.154 → 0.142 |
| KAPU BM25 only | 0.441 | 0.448 | 0.284 |
| Chunks (512 tokens, 64 overlap), BM25 | 0.485 | 0.448 | 0.299 |
| Chunks, vectors | 0.426 | 0.318 | 0.190 |

- Window vectors bring the KAPU vector ranking to the chunk baseline on the Rust book and Kubernetes, and the hybrid ranking to or above chunked BM25 there.
- On MDN they do not help: its 84,910 doors are mostly short sections, and a room and its own sections now match with the same window and take several of the first 10 places. Merging a room with its sections in the vector ranking is the next thing to try.
- The BM25 ranking and every result of format 1.0 files are unchanged: the summing order of the scores is the same.

## 10. Reproduce

```bash
python tools/collect_skills.py bench/korpusz/rooms           # the installed skills as rooms (local only)
kapu import <rooms folder> corpus.kapu --model models/potion-multilingual-128M-i8
kapu validate corpus.kapu --full
kapu bench corpus.kapu questions.jsonl --json                # recall@5/10, MRR@10 per mode
python tools/mcp_probe.py corpus.kapu questions.jsonl        # MCP latency and response sizes
python tools/kapu_mcp_call.py call kapu_search '{"file": "...", "query": "..."}'
```

Chapters 8 and 9 (public documentation): `tools/study/prepare_corpus.py` and `so_questions.py` prepare the corpora and questions, `scale_probe.py` measures a size, `chunk_baseline.py` the chunk baselines, `collect_results.py` writes `results/2026-10-23-study-pilot.json`, and `results_page.py` turns that into the results page.

@@@ test-vectors
# Test vectors (M1)

Generated by `tools/build_vectors.py`; never edit these files by hand (some
contain a CR or a control byte on purpose, and `.gitattributes` marks them
binary so git leaves every byte alone). `expected.json` records what each file
must produce; the Rust tests in `crates/kapu-core/tests/acceptance.rs` read it.

| File | Expected | What it exercises |
|---|---|---|
| `minta.kapu` | valid | 3 rooms, 4 sub-doors, 2 `link` edges, `@@@` escapes (0 and 1 backslash), a `## ` line inside a code fence, a `\|` in a title, one room with and one without a trailing LF, the untouched `kapu new` template as room `szoba`. Built without a model (`emb=none`), so FR-10/4 applies to every reader. |
| `E001-magic.kapu` | E001 | first line `%KAPX 1.1` |
| `E002-header.kapu` | E002 | byte 511 is a space instead of LF |
| `E003-cr.kapu` | E003 | a CR inside `#MAP` (header recomputed, nothing else wrong) |
| `E004-control.kapu` | E004 | ESC (0x1B) inside `#DESC` |
| `E005-span.kapu` | E005 | `desc=` length overstated by 7 bytes, so it overlaps `#GRAPH` |
| `E006-bodyhash.kapu` | E006 | `bodyhash=` field wrong, body intact |
| `E007-doorhash.kapu` | E007 | one door's fingerprint altered in `#DOCS` |
| `E008-id.kapu` | E008 | room id `Szoba` (uppercase) used consistently in every section |
| `E009-dangling.kapu` | E009 | `#INDEX` posting that points past the last `#DOCS` line |
| `E010-caption.kapu` | E010 | 201-character title in `#MAP` |
| `E011-hashprefix.kapu` | E011 | `bodyhash=sha256:...` |
| `edge-stale-same-length.kapu` | reader: `index: stale` | room 1 edited in place with equal length: fingerprint mismatch, retry, in-memory rebuild, edited text served |
| `edge-stale-shifted.kapu` | reader: `index: stale` | room 1 grew by one line: later positions are off, rebuild from the `@@@` lines |

`expected.json` also lists every door of the sample with its id, title, price
(`o200k_base` tokens), character count, offset, length, BLAKE3 fingerprint,
group and served text, so M2 and M3 can assert against them directly.

`../compat/minta-1.0.kapu` is the same sample in format 1.0 (postings by door id);
the reader must still read it (D140).

The decisions that pin the open points of the spec for these vectors are
D56-D66; `tools/build_vectors.py` is their executable form.

